{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f07e39d99b57bc5aff", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Ray Optics - Test 3", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e4a9"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e463", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e3fb", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["6162fb486ed13040ec84d13d"], "question": {"text": "<p>A spherical mirror is obtained as shown in the figure from a hollow glass sphere, if an object is positioned in front&nbsp;of the mirror, what will be the nature and magnification of the image of the object? (Figure down&nbsp;as schematic and not to scale)</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fdd15d9ae-2714-4162-a3ed-be7ba83e4a53-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 307px; height: 185px;\" />&nbsp;</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "9b6344", "text": "Inverted, real and magnified", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e3fc"}, {"id": "75752c", "text": "Erect, virtual and magnified", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e3fd"}, {"id": "9314fc", "text": "Erect, virutual and unmagnified", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e3fe"}, {"id": "863c2e", "text": "Inverted, real and unmagnified", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e3ff"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e400", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4756"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eda"], "question": {"text": "A transparent block A having refractive index $\\mu=1.25$ is surrounded by another medium of refractive index $\\mu=1.0$ as shown in figure. A light ray is incident on the flat face of the block with incident angle $\\theta$ as shown in figure. What is the maximum value of $\\theta$ for which light suffers total internal reflection at the top surface of the block?<br/><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F7EQnaKMjdTVstBR849Nf3dyadKdZbzYZnVWoH2DO88A.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/>"}, "options": [{"id": "c9d7cb", "text": "$\\tan ^{-1}(4 / 3)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e401"}, {"id": "5b9229", "text": "$\\tan ^{-1}(3 / 4)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e402"}, {"id": "bcdf20", "text": "$\\sin ^{-1}(3 / 4)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e403"}, {"id": "065522", "text": "$\\cos ^{-1}(3 / 4)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e404"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e405", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b4f"], "question": {"text": "Light enters from air into a given medium at an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> with interface of the air-medium surface. After refraction, the light ray is deviated through an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> from its original direction. The refractive index of the medium is :"}, "options": [{"id": "2f0c77", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>732</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e406"}, {"id": "0ebd39", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>333</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e407"}, {"id": "e1d6da", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>414</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e408"}, {"id": "a80dca", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>732</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e409"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e40a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9250"], "question": {"text": "The eye can be regarded as a single refracting surface. The radius of curvature of this surface is equal to that of the cornea <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.8</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi>mm</mi><mo>)</mo></math>. This surface separates two media of refractive indices <math><mn>1</mn></math> and <math><mn>1.34</mn></math>. Calculate the distance from the refracting surface at which a parallel beam of light will come to focus."}, "options": [{"id": "b7ffe8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.0</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e40b"}, {"id": "2042e6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e40c"}, {"id": "e3b7a6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e40d"}, {"id": "6a5ef6", "text": "<math><mn>3.07 cm</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e40e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e40f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "If the refractive index of the material of a prism is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cot</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> is the angle of prism then the angle of minimum deviation will be"}, "options": [{"id": "05aacf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e410"}, {"id": "96ba60", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e411"}, {"id": "e277f7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e412"}, {"id": "ad80bc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e413"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e414", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b474f"], "concepts": ["60da9b4f62f042332c5382ed"], "question": {"text": "<p>In a reflecting telescope, a secondary mirror is used to:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "97e4c2", "text": "reduce the problem of mechanical support", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e415"}, {"id": "03f578", "text": "make chromatic aberration zero", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e416"}, {"id": "8a2845", "text": "move the eyepiece outside the telescopic tube", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e417"}, {"id": "d68230", "text": "remove spherical aberration", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e418"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e419", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3b"], "concepts": ["6162fb466ed13040ec84d055"], "question": {"text": "Two plane mirrors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are at right angle to each other shown. A point source <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> is placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></math> meter away from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively. The shortest distance between the images thus formed is : (Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn></math>)<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F34d8d307-7505-4f96-b61c-cc9868c885fa-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 254px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "5d9b27", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>a</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e41a"}, {"id": "afb937", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>10</mn></msqrt><mi>a</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e41b"}, {"id": "b8ea5e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>a</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e41c"}, {"id": "ddeeda", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e41d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e41e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e1"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a3"], "question": {"text": "A plano-convex lens having radius of curvature of first surface 2 cm exhibits focal length of $f_1$ in air. Another plano-convex lens with first surface radius of curvature 3 cm has focal length of $\\mathrm{f}_2$ when it is immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1.2 . If both the lenses are made of same glass of refractive index 1.5 , the ratio of $f_1$ and $f_2$ will be"}, "options": [{"id": "4ecf53", "text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e41f"}, {"id": "c0f725", "text": "$1: 3$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e420"}, {"id": "11eb5a", "text": "$3: 5$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e421"}, {"id": "139bf7", "text": "$2: 3$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e422"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e423", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["6117b88f67dbe2603c3e8095"], "question": {"text": "The focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi></math> is related to the radius of curvature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> of the spherical convex mirror by:"}, "options": [{"id": "b0a1ba", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>r</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e424"}, {"id": "9e51a7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>r</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e425"}, {"id": "b10d3a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e426"}, {"id": "488995", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>r</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e427"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e428", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["6162fb4c6ed13040ec84d408"], "question": {"text": "<p>A convex lens has power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>. It is cut into two halves along its principal axis. Further one piece (out of the two halves) is cut into two halves perpendicular to the principal axis (as shown in figures). Choose the incorrect option for the reported pieces.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F21882c04-d4ef-4557-a091-44fcd48c83d4-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 138px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "86cdcb", "text": "Power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e429"}, {"id": "69a335", "text": "Power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e42a"}, {"id": "a9d6fc", "text": "Power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e42b"}, {"id": "1f1fc5", "text": "Power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e42c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e42d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3b"], "concepts": ["617aa9daef3fe65ad8e829f4"], "question": {"text": "A point source of light, S is placed at a distance L in front of the center of plane mirror of width d which is hanging vertically on a wall. A man walks in front of the mirror along a line parallel to the mirror, at a distance 2L as shown below. The distance over which the man can see the image of the light source in the mirror is:<br /><br><br /><br><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fihqavtnppzcmnvmzvgsiygssgcmcuqazqfndpxbgncwogjrujg.png?alt=media\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "8d3e4a", "text": "<math><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e42e"}, {"id": "c95a48", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e42f"}, {"id": "6d771b", "text": "<math><mi>d</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e430"}, {"id": "6bbdfe", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e431"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e432", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b565d"], "question": {"text": "A vessel of depth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></math> is half filled with a liquid of refractive index <math><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> and the upper half with another liquid of refractive index <math><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> . The liquids are immiscible. The apparent depth of the inner surface of the bottom of the vessel will be"}, "options": [{"id": "70e451", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e433"}, {"id": "96dc87", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e434"}, {"id": "74fedd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e435"}, {"id": "a983f9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e436"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e437", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5e04"], "question": {"text": "A thin prism <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> with an angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and made of glass of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>54</mn></math> is combined with another prism <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> made from glass of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>72</mn></math> to produce dispersion without average deviation. The angle of prism <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is :"}, "options": [{"id": "ac0cb7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e438"}, {"id": "c77d55", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e439"}, {"id": "2524ec", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e43a"}, {"id": "de89ad", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e43b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e43c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b474f"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b22"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements : one is labeled as Assertion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and the other is labeled as Reason <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>.</p><p>Assertion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>: For a simple microscope, the angular size of the object equals the angular size of the image.</p><p>Reason <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>: Magnification is achieved as the small object can be kept much closer to the eye than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;and hence it subtends a large angle. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "f42c60", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;is false but&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e43d"}, {"id": "625b9a", "text": "Both&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> are true but&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is NOT the correct explanation of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e43e"}, {"id": "c0298c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;is true but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e43f"}, {"id": "6debcd", "text": "Both&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> are true and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is the correct explanation of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e440"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e441", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4756"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eda"], "question": {"text": "Critical angle of incidence for a pair of optical media is $45^{\\circ}$. The refractive indices of first and second media are in the ratio:"}, "options": [{"id": "5e133b", "text": "$1: \\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e442"}, {"id": "80da18", "text": "$\\sqrt{2}: 1$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e443"}, {"id": "0c1e8e", "text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e444"}, {"id": "998acc", "text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e445"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e446", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e1"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a3"], "question": {"text": "A convex lens (of focal length <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> ) and a concave mirror, having their principal axes along the same lines, are kept <math><mn>80</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> apart from each other. The concave mirror is to the right of the convex lens. When an object is kept at a distance of <math><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> to the left of the convex lens, its image remains at the same position even if the concave mirror is removed. The maximum distance of the object for which this concave mirror, by itself would produce a virtual image would be:"}, "options": [{"id": "6429fa", "text": "<math><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e447"}, {"id": "001284", "text": "<math><mn>25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e448"}, {"id": "961e54", "text": "<math><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e449"}, {"id": "0f3416", "text": "<math><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e44a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e44b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["6176f3f76581241c7072b25c"], "question": {"text": "Two identical objects are placed in front of convex mirror and concave mirror having same radii of curvature of 12 cm , at same distance of 18 cm from the respective mirrors. The ratio of sizes of the images formed by convex mirror and by concave mirror is :"}, "options": [{"id": "5ac9ce", "text": "$1 / 2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e44c"}, {"id": "1d37f5", "text": "2", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e44d"}, {"id": "d7d300", "text": "3", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e44e"}, {"id": "24df20", "text": "$1 / 3$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e44f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e450", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b30"], "question": {"text": "A thin lens made of glass (refractive index <math><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></math> ) of focal length <math><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> is immersed in a liquid of refractive index <math><mn>1.42</mn></math> . If its focal length in liquid is <math><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></math> , then the ratio <math><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is closest to the integer:"}, "options": [{"id": "8f110b", "text": "<math><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e451"}, {"id": "b17616", "text": "<math><mn>9</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e452"}, {"id": "012141", "text": "<math><mn>5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e453"}, {"id": "1c1500", "text": "<math><mn>17</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e454"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e455", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3b"], "concepts": ["6162fb466ed13040ec84d062"], "question": {"text": "A slanted object $A B$ is placed on one side of convex lens as shown in the diagram. The image is formed on the opposite side. Angle made by the image with principal axis is:<br/><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fnp-6_NsKXBkCHvphvEQ6pVN5mlU9msUvRkA3ATW_l-8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/>"}, "options": [{"id": "dd8888", "text": "$-\\frac{\\alpha}{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e456"}, {"id": "aa9934", "text": "$-45^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e457"}, {"id": "7f982a", "text": "$+45^{\\circ}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e458"}, {"id": "2d12bb", "text": "$-\\alpha$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e459"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e45a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["6162fb496ed13040ec84d24f"], "question": {"text": "A light wave is incident normally on a glass slab of refractive index <math><mn>1.5</mn><mo>.</mo></math> If <math><mn>4</mn><mi>%</mi></math> of light gets reflected and the amplitude of the&nbsp;electric field of the incident light is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math> then the amplitude of the electric field for the wave propagating in the glass medium will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "4dc147", "text": "<math><mn>30</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e45b"}, {"id": "58ce9f", "text": "<math><mn>6</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e45c"}, {"id": "4cd7e6", "text": "<math><mn>24</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e45d"}, {"id": "d7e7fb", "text": "<math><mn>10</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e45e"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:29:44.651Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:29:44.651Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e4a7"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e4a5", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e489", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e56813499d1e49588fa38e"], "question": {"text": "<p>A convex lens of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math> and focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>18</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;in air is immersed in water. The change in focal length of the lens will be_____<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>.&nbsp;</p><p>(Given refractive index of water<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>)</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "ef2d98", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e48a"}, {"id": "9c3cfb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e48b"}, {"id": "8f6283", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e48c"}, {"id": "554c6b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e48d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e48e", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e1"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a3"], "question": {"text": "<p>An object is placed at a distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> from a convex lens. A convex mirror of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is placed on another&nbsp;side of the lens at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> as shown in the figure. The image of the object coincides with the object.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F28b5cbdc-79a9-47cd-8df1-e2c79ed15c7e-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 550px; height: 148px;\" /></p><p>When the convex mirror is removed, a real and inverted image is formed at a position. The distance of the image from the object will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "db69d9", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e48f"}, {"id": "85610f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e490"}, {"id": "d76aea", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e491"}, {"id": "66a90e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e492"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e493", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "A ray of light suffers minimum deviation when incident on a prism having angle of the prism equal to $60^{\\circ}$. The refractive index of the prism material is $\\sqrt{2}$. The angle of incidence (in degrees) is ______."}, "options": [{"id": "f714c8", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e494"}, {"id": "68901e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e495"}, {"id": "7cef54", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e496"}, {"id": "288238", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e497"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e498", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3b"], "concepts": ["6162fb466ed13040ec84d05a"], "question": {"text": "<p>A light ray is incident, at an incident angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#952;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, on the system of two plane mirrors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> having an inclination angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>75</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> between them (as shown in figure). After reflecting from mirror <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> it gets reflected back by the mirror <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> with an angle of reflection <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. The total deviation of the ray will be _____ degree.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F932045b2-6633-43c7-84f1-b1d130c7c9c9-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 230px; height: 164px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "5e706c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e499"}, {"id": "f4489b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e49a"}, {"id": "9c6b80", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e49b"}, {"id": "3a11f9", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e49c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e49d", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["6162fb486ed13040ec84d13d"], "question": {"text": "When an object is kept at a distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> from a concave mirror, the image is formed at a distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>from the mirror. If the object is moved with a speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> , the speed (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>) with which image moves at that instant is"}, "options": [{"id": "0aad8e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e49e"}, {"id": "75dfce", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e49f"}, {"id": "4d5997", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e4a0"}, {"id": "3e72f7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e4a1"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283c623cf4b3ad8f125e5", "title": "JEE Numerical", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:27.056Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 5, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:29:44.737Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:29:44.737Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 1, "_id": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e4a8"}], "bonusSections": [], "packDetails": [], "paidFeatures": [], "tags": [], "timeRestrictionSlots": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T11:16:00.444Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-17T04:58:00.857Z", "testType": "PYQs as Chapter Tests", "isResumeBufferAllowed": true, "enableAccessibility": true, "maxAttemptableQuestion": 25, "analysisVideoThumbnail": "", "analysisVideoThumbnailDark": "", "shortTitle": "", "subjectDetails": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "title": "Physics", "shortName": "Phy"}], "testTimerType": "mm", "isMarkForReviewAvailable": true}, "temporaryTestDetail": {"_id": "6a4a1e704e144b238177934f", "testId": "69de21f07e39d99b57bc5aff", "userId": "6914cc9c4d7566258ee3a8fd", "startedAt": "2026-07-05T09:05:52.430Z", "lastAttemptedSection": null, "totalTimeLeft": 50, "bonusAttempt": false, "sections": [{"sectionId": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e463", "questions": [{"questionId": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e3fb", "markedOptions": [], "inputValue": null, "timeTaken": 0, "timeLeftWhenAttempted": null, "status": "notVisited", "_id": "6a4a1e704e144b2381779351", "uid": "6a4a1e704e144b2381779352"}, {"questionId": "69de4f582ee0e063d926e400", "markedOptions